---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Chapter from "../components/Chapter.astro";
import Offer from "../components/Offer.astro";
import RiseHeading from "../components/RiseHeading.astro";
import type { Line } from "../components/RiseHeading.astro";
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import pbiHero from "../assets/photos/DSC08846-Edit_cropped.jpg";
import BookCta from "../components/BookCta.astro";
import { mailtoFor } from "../config/site";

const contactSubject = "Power BI & Fabric - working together";
const contactMailto = mailtoFor(contactSubject);
const heroLines: Line[] = ["Working alongside", { before: "your Power BI ", em: "team", after: "." }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How I work with Power BI teams - burningsuit"
  description="Practical Power BI work, advice to the person leading it, and help taking over existing systems. How Alan works alongside your team, from the first call onwards."
  ogImage="/social/linkedin-power-bi.png"
  ogImageAlt="Power BI and Fabric, done with you, beside a field-notebook model diagram"
  ogImageWidth={1200}
  ogImageHeight={627}
  contactSubject={contactSubject}
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation">Tell me about the reporting you're responsible for and the work ahead. We can think through where some help would be useful</Fragment>

  <section class="page-hero pbi-hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <RiseHeading as="h1" kind="hero" label="Working alongside your Power BI team." lines={heroLines} />
        <p class="lead enter appear-300">We use the work your team needs to do to build its understanding of Power BI. I help people work through changes, understand why the model behaves as it does, and <strong>check the result</strong>.</p>
        <p class="hero-sig enter appear-450">If you lead the team, I can also be someone to think the decisions through with. You don't have to work in Power BI every day to be responsible for what comes out of it.</p>
        <BookCta src="power-bi-hero" mailtoHref={contactMailto} variant="hero" />
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-photo enter appear-300">
        <Picture src={pbiHero} alt="Alan in a red checked overshirt, smiling, in a narrow brick alley" formats={["avif"]} fallbackFormat="webp" widths={[400, 600, 800]} sizes="(min-width: 62rem) 36vw, 100vw" quality={70} loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <Chapter id="situations" kicker="Start with the work that matters">
    <p>We begin with what the reporting is for and what your team can already do. A new report, a change to an existing model and a handover can need quite different help. Understanding that gives us a useful place to start.</p>
    <div class="offer-table">
      <Offer id="deliver" title="Build and change your reporting">
        <p>Your team has something to deliver. We work through the decisions behind it, from what a number needs to mean to how the model should calculate it. Your people do the work with my help, so they understand the choices and can change them later.</p>
        <p>When a result looks wrong, we follow it back through the model. Finding the cause is useful in its own right; understanding how we found it helps the team deal with the next one.</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer id="skills" title="Learn what the work needs">
        <p>If an idea is missing or misunderstood, I teach it through a practical example. That might need a focused explanation while we're working, or a workshop with time to practise afterwards.</p>
        <p>If you're arranging training, we agree what people need to be able to do with it. For example, a team learning row-level security could apply it to a report and check that each audience sees the right data. That gives the learning a purpose and you a way to judge the result.</p>
      </Offer>
      <Offer id="handover" title="Understand a system you inherited">
        <p>A working system and a set of handover documents don't always give a team enough understanding to look after it. We trace how the reporting gets from source data to the numbers people use, then practise making and checking changes.</p>
        <p>This can happen alongside an external build or after handover. The <a class="u" href="/work/law-firm/">law-firm case study follows a team learning to investigate its new Fabric system</a>.</p>
      </Offer>
    </div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="how-the-work-runs" kicker="Working through it together">
    <p>A session starts with a question or a piece of work. We might sketch how a model fits together, investigate a calculation, or work through an example of something the team needs to understand. I explain the reasoning as we go, so people can use it when I'm no longer on the call.</p>
    <p>We can use representative examples or your own systems. We choose that together, according to the work, what the team is ready for and your data rules. Access to live data isn't a prerequisite.</p>
    <p>The team needs time to try things between sessions. That is often when the useful questions appear: the example made sense, but the real data has a complication we haven't dealt with yet.</p>
    <Offer id="backup" title="Somewhere to bring the next question">
      <p>Office hours give the team a place to bring a stubborn calculation, talk through a proposed change or check its understanding. Email support can cover questions between calls. We agree the amount of support around the work and review it as the team needs less help.</p>
    </Offer>
    <p>The <a class="u" href="/work/contact-centre/">contact-centre case study shows how this helped a lead build and maintain her own reporting</a>.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="second-opinion" kicker="Support for the person leading it">
    <p>You might need to decide whether an architecture proposal makes sense, what a new hire needs to know, or whether the team is ready to take over a system. Those are decisions about the work and the people doing it, as well as the technology.</p>
    <p>I can help you work out what you need to understand personally, what knowledge the team should hold, and where too much depends on one person. We can look at a proposal together and identify the questions that need answering before you sign it off.</p>
    <p>A regular conversation of your own can sit alongside the team's sessions or be arranged separately. It gives you room to think about the direction of the work as well as the next thing to deliver.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="ai" kicker="AI in the work">
    <p>If your team is trying Copilot or coding agents, we can work through a task together and look at what the tool produces. The useful question is what your people need to understand to judge and maintain that work.</p>
    <p>That includes deciding which tasks to delegate, what information the tool can access, and how the team will check its output. A generated measure still needs someone who understands what the number is meant to represent.</p>
    <p>I explore what this means for the people responsible in <a class="u" href="/writing/we-are-all-middle-management-now/">We're all middle management now</a>, an essay about delegation, judgement and Power BI.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="pricing" kicker="Start with a conversation">
    <p>The first step is a free 60-minute call. Tell me about the reporting, the team and what you're trying to do. We can work out where help would be useful and whether it makes sense to work together.</p>
    <p>That conversation also tells us whether we need to spend more time understanding the work before agreeing an engagement. If we do, we can scope Discovery around what we need to find out.</p>
    <div class="section-links"><a class="cta ghost" href="/hour/">About the free hour ▸</a></div>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="discovery" kicker="Discovery, shaped around the question">
    <p>Discovery is <strong>£1,950 excluding VAT</strong>. Before booking it, we agree what we need to understand and what would be useful for you to take away.</p>
    <p>Depending on the work, that could include:</p>
    <ul class="discovery-examples">
      <li>An initial set of reporting requirements, based on the questions people need the reporting to answer.</li>
      <li>A roadmap for the reporting work, or for the skills the team will need to deliver and maintain it.</li>
      <li>An assessment of whether the ambition fits the team and the time available.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These are examples, rather than a fixed bundle. You keep what we produce. If that gives you enough to carry on internally, we can stop there.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter id="ongoing" kicker="Support as the work develops">
    <p>I'll give you a fixed quote for the work and sessions we agree, including preparation, materials and support between calls. We choose the mix of work with the team and conversations with you according to what will help.</p>
    <p>For substantial ongoing work with a team, <strong>£3,000 to £6,000 a month excluding VAT</strong> is a guide. Lighter support is available below that range and quoted separately, including fewer sessions or a regular second opinion.</p>
    <p>You can book a month at a time, or agree a single fixed quote for at least three months up front. Discovery is credited only against an upfront booking of at least three months.</p>
    <p>As the team becomes more capable, we can reduce the time and cost together. A monthly arrangement can stop when it stops being useful.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <section class="cta-band" aria-label="Book an hour">
    <div class="wrap"><BookCta src="power-bi-pricing" mailtoHref={contactMailto}>Bring the reporting question you're working on. We'll spend an hour thinking through a useful starting point; the call is free.</BookCta></div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer"></div>
</BaseLayout>
